<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kazybek Bi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1667">1667</a>–1764</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kazybek Bi was a Kazakh judge, orator and diplomat of the Middle Juz, one of the three great biys traditionally linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauke-khan">Tauke Khan</a>'s law code, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhety-zhargy">Zhety Zhargy</a> or 'Seven Charters', together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tole-bi">Tole Bi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aiteke-bi">Aiteke Bi</a>. Famed for his eloquence and called 'the goose-voiced', he negotiated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> during their wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>. His memory as a wise judge remains strong in Kazakhstan.</p></div>
